34
00:02:27,956 --> 00:02:29,774
Okay, let's start from data pipeline.

35
00:02:29,928 --> 00:02:34,976
We could define it as a system
that ingests raw data from multiple

36
00:02:34,976 --> 00:02:40,692
sources, transforms it, and loads
it into a data store for analysis

37
00:02:40,692 --> 00:02:42,250
and operational use, for example.

38
00:02:42,389 --> 00:02:47,435
So we have three stages:
ingestion, processing, and storage.

39
00:02:47,738 --> 00:02:50,033
So why do we care about it?

40
00:02:50,356 --> 00:02:56,128
Because data pipelines are the foundation
for basically every data project.

41
00:02:56,475 --> 00:03:00,812
Analytics, dashboards, machine learning,
fraud detection, observability.

42
00:03:01,002 --> 00:03:05,845
That's exactly why once they
work, everyone wants one.

43
00:03:07,436 --> 00:03:07,837
Okay.

44
00:03:07,899 --> 00:03:12,310
Data pipelines come in
three main shapes, right?

45
00:03:12,476 --> 00:03:15,512
So depending on how and
when the data moves.

46
00:03:16,102 --> 00:03:21,676
First, batch processing, where data is
handled in scheduled and periodic batches.

47
00:03:22,060 --> 00:03:26,000
It's well-suited to large
volumes of historical data or

48
00:03:26,017 --> 00:03:28,781
data that is not time-sensitive.

49
00:03:29,192 --> 00:03:32,498
So this is the classic ETL world.

50
00:03:32,912 --> 00:03:37,978
then we have the streaming, where
the data is processed in real

51
00:03:38,015 --> 00:03:39,928
time as it's generated, right?

52
00:03:40,064 --> 00:03:42,696
So you can analyze and act immediately.

53
00:03:43,126 --> 00:03:46,555
think about, fraud detection,
sensor data, social media

54
00:03:46,564 --> 00:03:47,990
monitoring, something like that.

55
00:03:48,487 --> 00:03:52,560
We can also combine both in the
Lambda architecture built to

56
00:03:52,561 --> 00:03:56,147
get the, the, the best of each.

57
00:03:56,559 --> 00:03:58,957
So it has three layers.

58
00:03:59,013 --> 00:04:02,587
The batch layer recomputes the
full data set from scratch.

59
00:04:02,683 --> 00:04:04,032
It's accurate but slow.

60
00:04:04,636 --> 00:04:10,361
The speed or streaming layer handles
the new data in real time to gover- to

61
00:04:10,363 --> 00:04:12,571
cover the gap from the latest batch.

62
00:04:12,897 --> 00:04:14,602
It's fast, but only recent.

63
00:04:15,384 --> 00:04:21,272
The serving layer merges the two, so
a query extracts the full history,

64
00:04:21,470 --> 00:04:23,683
plus the, the latest changes.

65
00:04:23,894 --> 00:04:27,797
So the downside is the complexity, right?

66
00:04:27,829 --> 00:04:32,814
Because you have two pla-
pipelines for the same data.

67
00:04:33,740 --> 00:04:40,897
The modern answer to that is the stream
house, which unifies the streaming

68
00:04:40,899 --> 00:04:46,450
and batch on a lake house, so you
keep one pipeline for both layer.

69
00:04:47,192 --> 00:04:49,988
Then we have the data
integration pipelines.

70
00:04:50,002 --> 00:04:56,130
These bring data together from
many sources into a single view,

71
00:04:57,140 --> 00:05:02,129
cleaning and transforming raw
data before storing it centrally.

72
00:05:02,279 --> 00:05:07,054
That's definitely useful when
different systems store their data

73
00:05:07,075 --> 00:05:13,070
in different formats So what do
people actually build with them?

74
00:05:13,503 --> 00:05:16,965
Analytics and BI is a
classic use case, right?

75
00:05:16,989 --> 00:05:20,918
With dashboards, reports, doc
analysis, but we have also data

76
00:05:20,919 --> 00:05:25,628
integration with many sources
to a warehouse or a data lake.

77
00:05:26,111 --> 00:05:30,176
machine learning where pipelines
supply and keep features fresh.

78
00:05:30,650 --> 00:05:36,650
real-time use cases like fraud detection,
reacting to events as they happen.

79
00:05:37,112 --> 00:05:41,108
data observability, operational
sync, keeping caches, search

80
00:05:41,108 --> 00:05:43,919
indexes, downstream services updated.

81
00:05:44,372 --> 00:05:50,196
the last group, the real-time
operational one, is exactly where

82
00:05:50,214 --> 00:05:54,484
change data capture shines, and
that's where we are going next.

83
00:05:55,758 --> 00:06:00,917
Okay, so how do we feed all
these use cases without reloading

84
00:06:01,388 --> 00:06:03,104
everything over and over?

85
00:06:03,520 --> 00:06:06,585
The answer is change data capture or CDC.

86
00:06:06,861 --> 00:06:08,338
The idea is simple.

87
00:06:08,379 --> 00:06:14,779
Instead of scanning entire tables
continuously to find what's new, CDC

88
00:06:14,971 --> 00:06:21,033
records every row-level change as it
happens, each insert, update, delete.

89
00:06:21,094 --> 00:06:26,434
So it then stream only those
cha-changes downstream.

90
00:06:26,771 --> 00:06:31,402
So rather than moving the whole dataset,
you move just what actually changed

91
00:06:31,645 --> 00:06:33,722
at the moment it changes, right?

92
00:06:33,830 --> 00:06:40,428
So that keeps every downstream system in
sync with the source in near real time and

93
00:06:40,429 --> 00:06:43,099
without the cost of full reload, for sure.

94
00:06:44,587 --> 00:06:47,167
So why not just run a big job?

95
00:06:48,052 --> 00:06:53,611
The problem is that traditional
ETL keeps scanning the same data

96
00:06:53,645 --> 00:06:58,939
over and over again, even when only
few rows has, have changed, right?

97
00:06:58,941 --> 00:07:03,117
So that means, more compute,
more load on the database, and

98
00:07:03,136 --> 00:07:05,461
data that is not fully updated.

99
00:07:05,886 --> 00:07:08,749
So CDC takes a different
approach, approach.

100
00:07:09,012 --> 00:07:14,039
Instead of reprocessing everything,
it capture only the changes

101
00:07:14,270 --> 00:07:16,424
and streams them downstream.

102
00:07:16,650 --> 00:07:24,403
So that dramatically reduce the delay
between the change happening and that

103
00:07:24,416 --> 00:07:27,160
change becoming available to consumers.

104
00:07:27,635 --> 00:07:33,908
So another important advantage is that
the CDC usually reads the transaction log

105
00:07:34,339 --> 00:07:37,333
rather than querying the tables directly.

106
00:07:37,335 --> 00:07:41,308
So the impact on the source
database is usually very low.

107
00:07:41,957 --> 00:07:47,394
And because changes are continuously
replicated, the CDC also enables, things

108
00:07:47,438 --> 00:07:54,690
like zero downtime migrations, database
upgrade, and replication between systems.

109
00:07:55,692 --> 00:07:59,923
So what is made possible
by that freshness?

110
00:08:00,109 --> 00:08:04,797
For sure, real-time dashboards,
recommendation engines, operational

111
00:08:04,797 --> 00:08:09,882
analytics are different use
cases, but with same requirement.

112
00:08:10,027 --> 00:08:13,327
Data needs to move as changes happen.

113
00:08:13,706 --> 00:08:17,308
So that's exactly what CDC provides

114
00:08:18,308 --> 00:08:21,355
There are three main ways to do the CDC.

115
00:08:21,632 --> 00:08:25,070
query-based polling with the
query executed using a, a

116
00:08:25,148 --> 00:08:26,963
timestamp or version column.

117
00:08:27,125 --> 00:08:32,465
It's simple and works on legacy
system, but it can miss deletes

118
00:08:33,049 --> 00:08:35,462
and gets expensive at scale.

119
00:08:36,013 --> 00:08:42,494
Trigger-based CDC uses, database triggers
to record changes into shadow tables.

120
00:08:42,866 --> 00:08:49,122
It's precise and flexible, but it adds,
write overhead to every transaction.

121
00:08:49,602 --> 00:08:55,855
And the log-based CDC, which reads the
database own transaction log, the binlog,

122
00:08:55,912 --> 00:08:57,749
the WAL, the redo logs, for instance.

123
00:08:57,752 --> 00:09:03,847
So it capture every operation in real
time with minimal impact on the source.

124
00:09:04,264 --> 00:09:09,125
That's the most common approach
Debezium takes, and it's why

125
00:09:09,155 --> 00:09:11,332
Debezium is such a good fit.

126
00:09:12,332 --> 00:09:14,221
Let's talk about Debezium.

127
00:09:14,262 --> 00:09:21,058
Debezium is the open source project that
makes the log-based CDC practical, right?

128
00:09:21,235 --> 00:09:28,217
So it reads the database own transaction
log, the same file that database uses

129
00:09:28,221 --> 00:09:33,129
for replication and recovery, and
streams changes from all the major

130
00:09:33,170 --> 00:09:35,625
databases to pretty much anywhere.

131
00:09:35,841 --> 00:09:40,367
So it's robust, widely adopted,
and it's the engine underneath

132
00:09:40,530 --> 00:09:41,942
everything else in this talk.

133
00:09:43,634 --> 00:09:45,218
So why Debezium?

134
00:09:45,258 --> 00:09:47,988
For sure it's log-based
and complete, right?

135
00:09:48,023 --> 00:09:53,611
So it reads the transaction log, so
it puts minimum load on the source,

136
00:09:54,109 --> 00:09:59,439
and it capture every insert, update,
and delete in commit order with

137
00:09:59,493 --> 00:10:04,011
before and after state, so without
any changes to your application.

138
00:10:04,875 --> 00:10:10,521
Then it's reliable by design because
offsets are recorded, so a restart

139
00:10:10,749 --> 00:10:12,854
resumes exactly where it left off.

140
00:10:13,497 --> 00:10:17,877
Your consumers can go down for
maintenance, for example, and never miss

141
00:10:17,878 --> 00:10:23,250
an event, and t- at least once delivery
is the floor, not the aspiration.

142
00:10:23,944 --> 00:10:29,397
It's broad but unified because we have
one event format and schema across

143
00:10:29,569 --> 00:10:35,459
MySQL, Postgres, Oracle, SQL Server,
MongoDB, DB2, Cassandra, and more.

144
00:10:35,491 --> 00:10:38,968
So you learn it once
and reuse it everywhere.

145
00:10:39,623 --> 00:10:44,816
This is, also, project with a solid
and extensive community, right?

146
00:10:44,829 --> 00:10:48,101
That contributes to the
project in an active way.

147
00:10:48,361 --> 00:10:53,577
It's also flexible in terms of
deployment and technology option.

148
00:10:54,577 --> 00:10:57,377
Debezium is a whole ecosystem.

149
00:10:57,481 --> 00:11:03,094
At the center is Debezium itself, and
around it, around it several areas.

150
00:11:03,322 --> 00:11:09,237
So source connectors capture changes
from the many kinds of database.

151
00:11:09,690 --> 00:11:15,740
Multiple way, to you actually run a
connector, like the embedded engine, the

152
00:11:15,743 --> 00:11:18,131
Debezium server, the Quarkus extension.

153
00:11:18,602 --> 00:11:23,106
we have also a dedicated CLI for
managing configuration and pipeline.

154
00:11:23,342 --> 00:11:26,346
We have SMT, to shape events, in flight.

155
00:11:26,848 --> 00:11:32,699
There is also a growing AI side
with Debezium AI and Pi Debezium AI.

156
00:11:33,039 --> 00:11:37,721
And for deployment there is,
the Debezium operator and on, on

157
00:11:37,765 --> 00:11:43,119
top of it, the Debezium platform
There are a few ways to run those

158
00:11:43,132 --> 00:11:44,946
connector, as we already mentioned.

159
00:11:45,186 --> 00:11:51,279
The classic one is Kafka Connect, which
is our bread and butter, let me say, and

160
00:11:51,480 --> 00:11:53,894
needs a Kafka Connect cluster for sure.

161
00:11:54,342 --> 00:11:59,864
Then, then we have the Debezium,
server that is a lightweight

162
00:11:59,869 --> 00:12:04,310
standalone runtime for when you
don't want or don't need Kafka.

163
00:12:04,775 --> 00:12:09,554
and then there is a Debezium platform,
the same connector, same capabilities,

164
00:12:09,582 --> 00:12:15,367
but packaged for self-service at
scale, so compatible with Kubernetes,

165
00:12:15,384 --> 00:12:17,828
but also with off-basis deployments.

166
00:12:18,891 --> 00:12:20,002
Here is the catch.

167
00:12:20,634 --> 00:12:25,106
shipping even one pipeline means
understanding the whole stack.

168
00:12:25,540 --> 00:12:30,736
CDC concept like snapshot, offset,
and schema changes, how to set up the

169
00:12:30,737 --> 00:12:35,363
database, WAL or binlog replication
slot, the right permission.

170
00:12:35,760 --> 00:12:41,230
Debezium itself, its connector, SMTs,
converter, the dozen of connector

171
00:12:41,261 --> 00:12:47,121
properties you have to get right, how
to deploy it, how to operate, monitor,

172
00:12:47,126 --> 00:12:50,137
how to handle failures and upgrades.

173
00:12:50,160 --> 00:12:55,083
So expecting every single team
to learn all of this just to move

174
00:12:55,102 --> 00:12:57,231
their data simply doesn't scale.

175
00:12:58,832 --> 00:13:04,101
So the natural solution is don't
spread all that knowledge across

176
00:13:04,102 --> 00:13:08,132
every team, but concentrate it
in one place, the platform team.

177
00:13:08,603 --> 00:13:13,795
They own CDC, the database
setup, Debezium, connector

178
00:13:13,836 --> 00:13:15,833
configuration, and operations.

179
00:13:16,025 --> 00:13:22,277
So teams just ask for a pipeline with
related constraints, and the platform

180
00:13:22,278 --> 00:13:25,069
team takes care of the hard part, right?

181
00:13:25,574 --> 00:13:28,510
And these work really well at first.

182
00:13:28,788 --> 00:13:31,700
But watch what happens as adoption grows.

183
00:13:32,936 --> 00:13:34,699
Here is the real problem.

184
00:13:34,806 --> 00:13:39,737
Once CDC is popular, the hard
part is not CDC anymore, but

185
00:13:39,762 --> 00:13:41,224
it's everything around it.

186
00:13:41,789 --> 00:13:47,688
Every team that wants a pipeline has
to go through the platform team, review

187
00:13:47,690 --> 00:13:52,088
the config, deploy the connector,
watch it, fix it when it br- when,

188
00:13:52,149 --> 00:13:54,521
when it breaks, answer the ticket.

189
00:13:54,605 --> 00:13:58,656
So one team sits in the
middle of everyone's work,

190
00:13:59,016 --> 00:14:00,900
and it doesn't scale, right?

191
00:14:00,946 --> 00:14:05,731
Because the people wait, the platform
team could burn out and become a

192
00:14:05,731 --> 00:14:12,061
bottleneck The Debezium platform tackles
this by raising an abstraction level.

193
00:14:12,242 --> 00:14:17,301
So instead of everyone editing a
raw connector JSON, you can define

194
00:14:17,380 --> 00:14:22,919
reusable building blocks, sources,
destinations, and transformation.

195
00:14:23,077 --> 00:14:28,811
So a pipeline is simply a combination
of these while also allowing for any

196
00:14:28,813 --> 00:14:31,556
kind of tuning and customization.

197
00:14:31,920 --> 00:14:37,026
So the idea is to create, an abstraction
level that serves the most common

198
00:14:37,026 --> 00:14:43,291
use cases and make, make specific
behaviors easy and ergonomic to apply.

199
00:14:43,446 --> 00:14:47,995
So this is what transforms a mere
collection of configurations into

200
00:14:47,995 --> 00:14:53,173
a product With that abstraction
in place, the roles change, right?

201
00:14:53,198 --> 00:14:58,051
Because the platform team, no longer
deploys, every pipeline by hand.

202
00:14:58,270 --> 00:15:02,183
It handles the building blocks
and sets the guardrails, right?

203
00:15:02,422 --> 00:15:08,237
Data engineers create and manage their
own pipelines through the platform, and

204
00:15:08,237 --> 00:15:13,292
the platform handles the deployment,
operations, monitoring, and observability.

205
00:15:13,431 --> 00:15:15,231
So the bottleneck is gone.

206
00:15:16,438 --> 00:15:21,306
And everything lives in one place
because you can create a pipeline

207
00:15:21,306 --> 00:15:23,714
in the UI or using our CLI.

208
00:15:23,856 --> 00:15:31,020
The platform deploys and runs it, and
you can get, health, status, metrics

209
00:15:31,026 --> 00:15:33,007
for every pipeline out of the box.

210
00:15:33,276 --> 00:15:38,688
So instead of digging through the logs
across cluster, both the platform team

211
00:15:38,762 --> 00:15:43,214
and the data engineers see the state of
every pipeline from a single console.

212
00:15:44,666 --> 00:15:47,712
Okay, let's make it
concrete with a short demo.

213
00:15:47,771 --> 00:15:53,363
let's suppose we have a, a single Postgres
inventory database and three teams

214
00:15:53,534 --> 00:15:56,009
that, each need the data differently.

215
00:15:56,263 --> 00:16:01,633
analytics, wants clean, SQL rows,
so a JDBC, pipeline upsert into

216
00:16:01,634 --> 00:16:03,379
a separate warehouse database.

217
00:16:03,968 --> 00:16:09,069
the integration team just wants row change
events pushed out to an external HTTP,

218
00:16:09,276 --> 00:16:13,205
HTTP service vi-via, via w-web hook.

219
00:16:13,623 --> 00:16:18,738
And another team wants current
product and stock state in a cache,

220
00:16:19,015 --> 00:16:21,442
so the pipeline writes to Redis.

221
00:16:21,916 --> 00:16:27,712
all three share one source data store,
but get their own replication slot.

222
00:16:27,820 --> 00:16:29,654
So they are fully independent.

223
00:16:30,122 --> 00:16:35,127
I build the, the pipelines live
in the UI, and then we watch

224
00:16:35,263 --> 00:16:37,667
how the platform manage them.

225
00:16:38,942 --> 00:16:41,531
Quick look under the hood of my demo.

226
00:16:41,585 --> 00:16:45,492
I will use, Debezium platform
installed with Helm and deployed

227
00:16:45,512 --> 00:16:48,122
using a local Kind Kubernetes cluster.

228
00:16:48,402 --> 00:16:54,665
The platform has three-part, the stage UI,
where engineers build, build pipelines.

229
00:16:55,079 --> 00:17:00,612
The conductor, which is the control
